<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amenemhat IV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1786–1777 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a> · Also known as Amenemhet IV</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amenemhat IV was the penultimate king of the Twelfth Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, reigning for about nine years in the late nineteenth century BCE. He continued the works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii">Amenemhat III</a>, completing the temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medinet-madi">Medinet Madi</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Faiyum</a>, and sent expeditions to the turquoise mines of Sinai; the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> received gifts bearing his name. He was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobekneferu">Sobekneferu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iv.jpg" alt="Amenemhat IV" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juan R. Lazaro · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amenemhat_IV_upclose.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Amenemhat IV was the seventh and penultimate king of the late Twelfth Dynasty. His mother was a woman named Hetepti, whose only known attestation is an inscription in the temple of Renenutet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medinet-madi">Medinet Madi</a>, where she is called King's Mother but not King's Wife or King's Daughter. Manetho made him a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii">Amenemhat III</a>, but he is never called King's Son, so some historians think he was a grandson. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-ryholt">Kim Ryholt</a> has proposed that Hetepti was married to another man before Amenemhat III and that Amenemhat IV was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobekneferu">Sobekneferu</a>'s stepbrother.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He first ruled as a junior coregent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii">Amenemhat III</a>. Monuments with the two names side by side attest the coregency, which most scholars think lasted two years. The Turin Canon gives him a reign of 9 years, 3 months and 27 days, and Africanus's epitome of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a> gives eight years. His rule seems to have been peaceful. Four expeditions to the turquoise mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serabit-el-khadim">Serabit el-Khadim</a> in the Sinai are dated to it, the last in his ninth year and possibly the last of the Middle Kingdom. In his second year the assistant treasurer Sahathor led an expedition for amethyst to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadi-el-hudi">Wadi el-Hudi</a>.</p><p>A stela of year 7 was found at Berenice on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>. An ostracon from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mersa-gawasis">Wadi Gawasis</a> mentions an expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Punt</a> in year 8 under the royal scribe Djedy. Nilometer records at Kumna in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> for years 5 to 7 show that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> kept its presence there. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> an obsidian and gold chest and a jar lid bore his name. He completed the temple of Renenutet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobek">Sobek</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medinet-madi">Medinet Madi</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii">Amenemhat III</a> had begun, which Zahi Hawass called the only intact temple surviving from the Middle Kingdom, and he finished a shrine at the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a> in the Sinai.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His tomb has not been identified. The ruined Southern Mazghuna pyramid is a possibility, as are a pyramid at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dahshur">Dahshur</a> beside that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-ii">Amenemhat II</a> and, less likely, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii">Amenemhat III</a>'s first pyramid. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobekneferu">Sobekneferu</a>, daughter of Amenemhat III, succeeded him and ended the Twelfth Dynasty, which gave way to a much weaker Thirteenth Dynasty. Ryholt and Aidan Dodson see its first two kings, Sobekhotep I and Sonbef, as his sons, but Julien Siesse disputes this. Asiatic immigration into the Delta, begun under his predecessor, accelerated, and about 80 years after his reign the administration seems to have collapsed completely, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-intermediate-period">Second Intermediate Period</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amenemhat IV” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Middle Bronze Age local rulers such as Abishemu and his son Ipshemuabi adopted Egyptian forms, writing their names in hieroglyphs with the Egyptian title of a provincial governor and receiving gifts from the pharaohs Amenemhat III and <b>Amenemhat IV</b>, objects found in their rock-cut tombs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Twelfth Dynasty ended with <b>Amenemhat IV</b> and then Sobekneferu, the first woman known for certain to have taken the full titles of a king, around 1773 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
